      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In practice, this dynamic manifests when the veto power held by the US, UK, France, and their allies in the UN Security Council can instantly block resolutions aimed at the security or sovereignty of a minority or non-aligned nation without needing a security agreement. This allows dominant economic powers to enforce trade sanctions or shape security alliances that benefit multinational corporations while presenting a unified \"global good\" to the rest of the world.\n\nWhen a specific nation prioritizes its own sovereignty over \"international norms,\" it often fractures the consensus of voting blocs, causing development aid, debt relief, and diplomatic support to stall for that country. The global framework fails to provide a credible alternative for these nations if their specific demands contradict the entrenched interests of these powerful states.",
      "source": "target",
